\sectiontitle{Project Abstract (2 pts)}
\vspace{0.12cm}
\answerbox{%
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=4mm,itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]
\item Secure CBT is a computer-based test question-paper system built around a single idea: a paper that leaks should still be traceable back to exactly where it leaked from.
\item It targets the three points where a real exam paper is actually vulnerable: when the paper is set by teachers, when it is distributed down the exam hierarchy, and at the exam centre itself before the test begins.
\item Rather than adding tracing as an afterthought (access logs, attendance records, duty rosters), the system builds traceability directly into the encryption: every node in the distribution hierarchy holds its own uniquely derived key, via hierarchical XOR key-chaining combined with AES-256, so a leaked ciphertext is its own fingerprint.
\item Phase 1, the paper-setting pipeline (CSV upload, validation, question storage, paper generation, compression, and encryption), is built and running. Phase 2, secure distribution across the server hierarchy, is in progress. Phase 3, exam-centre decryption and leak tracing, is planned.
\end{itemize}}
\vspace{0.70cm}

\sectiontitle{Updated Project Approach and Architecture (2 pts)}
\vspace{0.25cm}
\begin{tcolorbox}[breakable,boxrule=0.5pt,colframe=black!60,colback=white,arc=0pt,left=6pt,right=6pt,top=6pt,bottom=6pt]
\fontsize{11}{13}\selectfont
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=4mm,itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]
\item The system is written in C++17 for all core data structures and algorithms, chosen for fine-grained control over memory and performance, which matters when every node in the hierarchy has to run its own encryption and lookup logic.
\item OpenSSL provides the cryptographic primitives: AES-256 for encrypting the paper at each node, and SHA-256 for the tamper-evident transfer log, so the project relies on audited, standard implementations rather than hand-rolled cryptography.
\item A web frontend handles the teacher-facing side: question-bank upload, CSV validation feedback, and template download, so the backend never has to deal with malformed input directly, and teachers get immediate feedback instead of a silent failure.
\item Git/GitHub is used for version control, with all three members pushing to the shared repository, which also gives a clear history of who implemented which module.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Phase 1 (built) --- Paper Setting}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=4mm,itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]
\item Teachers upload a CSV question bank through the web screen, following the downloaded template, so no single teacher controls the final assembled paper and no manual compilation step is needed.
\item The CSV checker reads the header and every row. It rejects the file outright if the header does not exactly match the template, and otherwise reports row-level errors (row number, field, and problem) so the teacher can fix and re-upload without guessing what went wrong.
\item A file is accepted only once it contains at least one valid question; a header-only file is reported as empty rather than silently accepted as a zero-question paper.
\item Valid questions are stored in a hash map (QuestionBank) for O(1) lookup by ID, and indexed by topic in a second hash map (TopicIndex), so the PaperGenerator can pull candidate questions per topic without scanning the whole bank.
\item PaperGenerator selects N questions per topic using random selection without replacement, removing each chosen ID from the pool so it cannot repeat, which keeps the final paper balanced across topics rather than skewed toward whichever questions happen to be picked first.
\item The selected questions are serialised into a single byte stream and compressed using Huffman coding: a frequency table is built from the byte stream, a min-heap merges nodes into a Huffman tree, and the resulting prefix-code table replaces common byte patterns with shorter bit codes.
\item The compressed paper is then encrypted. Compression always runs before encryption, since encrypted data has no repeating patterns left for Huffman coding to exploit, so doing it the other way around would waste the compression step entirely.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Phase 2 (in progress) --- Secure Distribution}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=4mm,itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]
\item The distribution structure is an N-ary tree, implemented as a Left-Child Right-Sibling (LCRS) binary tree: each node stores a \texttt{firstChild} pointer and a \texttt{nextSibling} pointer, so any number of real children (states, districts, centres) can be represented with exactly two pointers per node instead of a variable-length child list.
\item The paper is broadcast down the tree breadth-first (level by level), so every node at one level receives its copy before the next level begins, and transfers only ever move from parent to child, which rules out any accidental back-flow by construction.
\item Each node's copy is encrypted independently using XOR key-chaining combined with AES-256: a node's key is derived from its parent's key XORed with the node's own secret, so decrypting at any level requires both pieces and no level in the chain can be skipped.
\item Keys are derived on demand rather than pre-computed and stored in bulk, so only a small secret value is kept per node, which keeps memory overhead flat even as the hierarchy grows to cover many states and districts.
\item An AVL tree indexes every node by ID, giving guaranteed O(log n) lookup of that node's secret, assigned key, and current status, even as nodes are added, which avoids the worst-case linear lookup a plain unbalanced tree could fall into.
\item Every transfer between nodes is appended to a hash-chained log: each entry stores the SHA-256 hash of the previous entry plus its own data, so changing or removing any past entry breaks every hash after it in the chain, making tampering immediately detectable rather than something discovered later by chance.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Phase 3 (planned) --- Exam Centre}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=4mm,itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]
\item The exam centre server authenticates each student terminal and downloads the encrypted paper package addressed specifically to that centre, so a terminal cannot pull a paper meant for a different centre.
\item Decryption is time-locked: the centre cannot read the paper before the scheduled exam start time, enforced by a time check, or by a min-heap Extract-Min when a single centre has to manage several exam slots and needs to know which one unlocks next.
\item Decryption at the terminal uses the centre's key together with the node's own secret, following the same key-chain established in Phase 2, so Phase 3 reuses the existing key scheme instead of introducing a separate one.
\item If an encrypted paper file ever surfaces publicly, it is matched directly against the AVL registry carried over from Phase 2 to identify the exact centre or terminal it was issued to, without needing any separate fingerprint database.
\end{itemize}
\end{tcolorbox}

